F['indication_text']='This NCCP monotherapy pathway covers postmenopausal women with ER-positive locally advanced or metastatic breast cancer that has relapsed or progressed on or after adjuvant anti-oestrogen treatment. Fulvestrant prescribed with capivasertib or a CDK4/6 medicine follows a separate combination regimen.'
F['agents'][0].update(mechanism='Binds the oestrogen receptor, blocks its signalling and helps the cell remove the receptor',visual=['Oestrogen receptors can drive cancer-cell growth','Fulvestrant binds and blocks the receptor','The number of receptors falls, reducing growth signals'],explanation='Fulvestrant is a selective oestrogen-receptor degrader. It blocks receptor activity and lowers the amount of receptor available in cancer cells. The slow-release injection keeps the medicine in the body between visits; the extra first-month dose helps establish treatment exposure.',watch='Report severe injection pain, numbness, weakness or pain travelling down the leg, unusual bleeding or bruising, jaundice and symptoms that interfere with daily life.')
F['monitoring_summary']='Before treatment, check blood count, kidney and liver function, the menopausal and treatment history, pregnancy exclusion and bleeding risk. Before injections, tell staff about anticoagulants, bruising, bleeding or previous nerve symptoms. Repeat liver tests when indicated; scans follow the agreed cancer-control plan. Tell anyone ordering an oestrogen blood test that you receive fulvestrant because some tests can give a misleadingly high result.'
F['consent_support']='Discuss the treatment aim, injection discomfort and possible nerve injury, bleeding or bruising, liver effects, flushes, joint symptoms and fatigue. Liver impairment or an existing modified dose requires an agreed prescriber and pharmacy plan. Where relevant, use effective contraception during treatment and for two years after the final dose under the EMA product information. Pregnancy and breastfeeding are excluded. Tell staff about blood thinners; do not stop them yourself.'
F['support']=[item('Make injection visits manageable','Discuss injection discomfort, flushes, joint or muscle symptoms, fatigue and nausea. Ask early for symptom help or practical support attending appointments.',['nccp','smpc']),item('Support medicines','Fulvestrant alone does not require routine steroid premedication, injections to raise white cells or a standard anti-sickness course. A combination medicine can add its own supportive-care needs.',['nccp']),item('Bleeding and nerve symptoms','Tell staff about blood thinners, unusual bruising or bleeding before an injection. Severe buttock pain, new numbness, weakness or pain down the leg needs prompt assessment.',['nccp','smpc']),item('Tests and appointments','Confirm the extra loading visit and later appointments, who books required blood tests and scans, and who reviews the results. Tell the laboratory team about fulvestrant if an oestrogen blood test is ordered.',['nccp','smpc'])]
F['logistics']['sections'] += [{'title':'Other cancer medicines','text':'If you also receive capivasertib or a CDK4/6 medicine, use the full combination plan for tablets, safety checks and side-effect advice. Fulvestrant-alone information cannot replace it.'},{'title':'Oestrogen blood tests','text':'Tell the clinician and laboratory that you receive fulvestrant. Some antibody-based tests can read falsely high; the team should confirm how a result was measured before changing treatment.'}]
F['monitoring_schedule']['rows'][0]['cells']=['Blood count, kidney and liver profile; review medicines and bleeding risk','Confirm Day 15 loading visit; assess injection tolerability','Liver profile when indicated; review bleeding and nerve symptoms','Clinical and imaging review of benefit and tolerability','Follow unresolved symptoms or test abnormalities']
F['monitoring_schedule']['rows'].append({'label':'Oestrogen blood tests','cells':['Tell the team that fulvestrant is planned','Some tests can read falsely high','Confirm a suitable test with the laboratory if needed','Do not change treatment on a misleading result','Tell later clinicians about prior fulvestrant']})
F['evidence']={'title':'Why fulvestrant is offered','year':2014,'url':'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24317176/','population':'Postmenopausal people with hormone-sensitive advanced breast cancer studied in endocrine-treatment trials.','primary_endpoint':'Control of cancer progression and survival, evaluated separately.','interpretation':'Fulvestrant can help control advanced hormone-sensitive breast cancer. Studies support the usual treatment schedule, while the choice of treatment depends on previous endocrine therapy and the individual cancer.','limitations':'A study comparing fulvestrant doses does not show that monotherapy is better than modern combination treatment. A separate study in people who had not received endocrine therapy found longer control of progression but no proven overall-survival advantage over anastrozole. These findings do not predict an individual outcome.','source_ids':['CONFIRM_OS','FALCON_PFS','FALCON_OS'],'metrics':[]}
